 the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – a construction with \\(n\\) tiles\nFor every row \\(i\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl([n]\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile whose sides lie on grid lines; it covers all squares of row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe rows are distinct, so the rectangles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_n\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery unit square that is not one of the uncovered squares belongs to exactly one of the \\(R_i\\).  \nThus \\(n\\) tiles suffice, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le n=2025.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – a rank argument\n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix  \n\n\\[\nM=(m_{ij}),\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\[4pt]\n0 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is uncovered}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are exactly the positions \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\); hence the matrix of uncovered squares is the permutation matrix  \n\n\\[\nP_\\sigma=(p_{ij}),\\qquad \np_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }j=\\sigma(i),\\\\\n0 & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAll other entries are covered, so  \n\n\\[\nM=J-P_\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) denotes the all‑ones matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Each tile is a rank‑1 matrix\n\nA tile occupies a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with the property that no uncovered square lies inside; equivalently \\(\\sigma(R)\\cap C=\\varnothing\\).  \nIts indicator matrix is 