h \\(14\\) tiles.**  \n   Choose \\(2025\\) distinct subsets \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{2025}\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\), each of size \\(7\\) (possible because \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432\\)).  \n   For each row \\(i\\) set \\(A_i=S_i\\) and for each column \\(i\\) set \\(B_i=\\overline{S_i}\\) (the complement).  \n   Then \\(A_i\\cap B_i=\\varnothing\\) and for \\(i\\neq j\\),\n\n   \\[\n   A_i\\cap B_j=S_i\\setminus S_j\\neq\\varnothing,\n   \\]\n\n   because two distinct \\(7\\)-subsets of a \\(14\\)-set cannot contain one another.  \n   For each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\) define a tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T_t=\\bigl\\{(i,j)\\mid t\\in S_i\\text{ and }t\\notin S_j\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The row set of \\(T_t\\) is \\(\\{i\\mid t\\in S_i\\}\\) and the column set is \\(\\{j\\mid t\\notin S_j\\}\\); they are disjoint, so \\(T_t\\) is a legitimate rectangle.  \n   By the above intersection property every off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) belongs to at least one \\(T_t\\), while no diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) belongs to any tile.  \n   Thus the \\(14\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_{14}\\) satisfy the required condition.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that fewer than \\(14\\) tiles are impossible; the construction shows that \\(14\\) tiles are sufficient. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(14\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles that leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column forces the set of uncovered squares to be a *permutation matrix*: after possibly permuting rows and columns we may assume that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) must be covered, and no tile may contain a cell of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Translation to a set system\n\nSuppose we use \\(k\\) tiles.  \nFor the \\(t\\)-th tile let  \n\n\\[\nR_t=\\{\\,i\\mid\\text{the tile occupies a square in row }i\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC_t=\\{\\,j\\mid\\text{the tile occupies a square in column }j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a tile cannot contain a diagonal cell, we have  \n\n\\[\nR_t\\cap C_t=\\varnothing\\qquad(t=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConversely, any pair of subsets \\((R_t,C_t)\\) satisfying (3) determines a rectangle\n\\(R_t\\times C_t\\) that can be used as a tile.\n\nDefine for each row (resp. column) the index sets  \n\n\\[\nA_i=\\{\\,t\\mid i\\in R_t\\,\\},\\qquad \nB_i=\\{\\,t\\mid i\\in C_t\\,\\}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe covering requirement “every off‑diagonal cell is covered” translates into  \n\n\\[\n\\forall\\,i\\neq j\\quad\\exists\\,t\\;(i\\in R_t\\ \\text{and}\\ j\\in C_t)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nA_i\\cap B_j\\neq\\varnothing . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (3) we obtain the two‑family condition  \n\n\\[\nA_i\\cap B_i=\\varnothing\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nA_i\\cap B_j\\neq\\varnothing\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   A lower bound for the number of tiles\n\nAssume a family \\(\\{A_i,B_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) satisfies (6).  \nIf for some distinct \\(i,j\\) we had \\(A_i\\subseteq A_j\\), then from (6)\n\n\\[\nA_j\\cap B_i\\supseteq A_i\\cap B_i=\\varnothing,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the requirement \\(A_j\\cap B_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).  \nThus **no set \\(A_i\\) is contained in another**; the family \\(\\{A_i\\}\\) is an *antichain* of subsets of the \\(k\\)-element set \\([k]=\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\nBy Sperner’s theorem the size of an antichain of subsets of a \\(k\\)-set is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le r\\le k}\\binom{k}{r}=\\binom{k}{\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nn\\le\\binom{k}{\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{13}{6}=1716<2025\\le\\binom{14}{7}=3432 .\n\\]\n\nHence any feasible covering must satisfy \\(k\\ge14\\).  \nSo **at least 14 tiles are necessary**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Construction with 14 tiles\n\nWe now show that 14 tiles are sufficient.\n\nBecause \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432>2025\\), we can select \\(2025\\) distinct subsets  \n\n\\[\nS_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_{2025}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,14\\},\n\\qquad |S_i|=7\\ \\text{for all }i. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\nA_i=S_i,\\qquad B_i=\\overline{S_i}=\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\setminus S_i .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(A_i\\cap B_i=\\varnothing\\).  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\), the two \\(7\\)-subsets \\(S_i\\) and \\(S_j\\) are distinct, and because they have the same cardinality neither can contain the other; therefore  \n\n\\[\nA_i\\cap B_j=S_i\\setminus S_j\\neq\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nThus the families \\(\\{A_i\\},\\{B_i\\}\\) satisfy (6).\n\nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,14\\}\\) define a rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_t=\\bigl\\{(i,j)\\mid t\\in S_i\\text{ and }t\\notin S_j\\bigr\\}\n      =\\bigl\\{i\\mid t\\in S_i\\bigr\\}\\times\\bigl\\{j\\mid t\\notin S_j\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe row set of \\(T_t\\) is \\(\\{i\\mid t\\in S_i\\}= \\{i\\mid t\\in A_i\\}\\) and the column set is \\(\\{j\\mid t\\notin S_j\\}= \\{j\\mid t\\in B_j\\}\\); they are disjoint, so \\(T_t\\) is a legitimate axis‑parallel tile.\n\nFor any off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) we have \\(t\\in S_i\\setminus S_j\\) for some \\(t\\) (by the property \\(A_i\\cap B_j\\neq\\varnothing\\)). That \\(t\\) places \\((i,j)\\) inside \\(T_t\\). Diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are never covered because \\(t\\) cannot simultaneously belong to \\(S_i\\) and its complement. Hence the fourteen rectangles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_{14}\\) cover **exactly** all cells except the diagonal ones.\n\nThus a covering with \\(k=14\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Minimality\n\nThe lower bound proved in §2 shows that no covering with fewer than 14 tiles can exist, while the construction in §3 provides a covering with exactly 14 tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely 14.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Explicit example for the smallest non‑trivial case  \n\nThe same method works for any \\(n\\) with \\(\\binom{k}{\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor}\\ge n\\).  \nFor instance, for a \\(5\\times5\\) board (\\(n=5\\)) we have \\(\\binom{4}{2}=6\\ge5\\); choose the five 2‑subsets of \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\)\n\n\\[\nS_1=\\{1,2\\},\\;S_2=\\{1,3\\},\\;S_3=\\{1,4\\},\\;S_4=\\{2,3\\},\\;S_5=\\{2,4\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the four tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_4\\) as above; they cover all off‑diagonal cells of the \\(5\\times5\\) board, leaving exactly one uncovered cell in each row and column. This concrete illustration confirms the construction for the smallest non‑zero case.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid under the given conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self‑Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have verified that the summary states the final answer in the required \\boxed{} format, that the method sketch outlines the lower‑bound argument (Sperner’s theorem) and the explicit construction (14 subsets of size 7), and that the detailed solution contains a complete, step‑by‑step proof of both the necessity of at least 14 tiles and the sufficiency of 14 tiles, with all mathematical statements in proper TeX notation. No extraneous commentary or incomplete attempts remain."
