htmost** square of that row inside \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\); therefore \\(L\\) contains exactly \\(N-1\\) such rightmost squares.\n\n*Lemma.*  A rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain at most one rightmost square.\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\subseteq L\\).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have \\(d\\le p(i)-1\\) (otherwise column \\(d\\) would be \\(\\ge p(i)\\) and the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) would lie inside \\(T\\)).  \nConsequently \\(d\\le\\min_{i\\in[a,b]}(p(i)-1)\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contained two rightmost squares, say \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\((k,p(k)-1)\\) with \\(p(i)-1<p(k)-1\\), then the inequality forces \\(d\\le p(i)-1<p(k)-1\\); thus column \\(p(k)-1\\) would not belong to \\(T\\), a contradiction. ∎  \n\nSince there are \\(N-1\\) rightmost squares and each rectangle covers at most one, any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(N-1\\) rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right region \\(R\\)\n\nAnalogously, for each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<N\\) the square \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is the **leftmost** square of that row inside \\(R\\).  \nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=N\\); thus \\(R\\) also contains precisely \\(N-1\\) leftmost squares.  \n\nThe same argument as in the previous lemma (now applied to the set of leftmost squares) shows that any rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one of them. Consequently at least \\(N-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nCombining the two independent parts we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{(total number of rectangles)}\\;\\ge\\;(N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus no arrangement of tiles can use fewer than \\(2N-2\\) rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); i.e., place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,N\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering \\(R\\)).*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,N-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):i<j\\le N\\},\n\\]  \nwhich has size \\(1\\times(N-i)\\).  \nThese \\(N-1\\) rectangles cover all squares with column index larger than the row index.\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering \\(L\\)).*  \nFor each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,N-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j):j<i\\le N\\},\n\\]  \nwhich has size \\((N-j)\\times1\\).  \nThese \\(N-1\\) rectangles cover all squares with row index larger than the column index.\n\nThe two families are disjoint (the first family occupies the strict upper‑triangular part, the second the strict lower‑triangular part) and together they cover every square of the board except the diagonal holes.  \nThe total number of tiles used is \\((N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2\\).\n\nThus the bound (1) is tight.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion for \\(N=2025\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(N=2025\\) into the formula \\(2N-2\\) yields\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
