t one critical square. They consider three cases: two left-neighbors, two above-neighbors, one left and one above. They argue that in each case, the rectangle would also contain the hole(s) adjacent to those critical squares, which is a contradiction. The proof seems valid. However, we need to check if there could be a rectangle that contains a left neighbor of a hole and a left neighbor of another hole but not contain the holes themselves. Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have holes at (i, p(i)) and (j, p(j)). The left neighbor of hole i is (i, p(i)-1). The left neighbor of hole j is (j, p(j)-1). If rectangle contains both left neighbor squares, then the rectangle includes rows i and j, and columns p(i)-1 and p(j)-1. Since the rectangle includes the columns from min(p(i)-1, p(j)-1) to max(p(i)-1, p(j)-1), and includes rows i and j, it also includes the rectangle spanned by those rows and columns. Does that guarantee that the holes (i, p(i)) and (j, p(j)) are inside? Let's examine more concretely.\n\nIf p(i)-1 < p(j)-1, then the rectangle includes column p(i)-1 and column p(j)-1. The rectangle's column interval includes all columns between them, i.e., includes column p(i). Since p(i) is between p(i)-1 and p(j)-1 (if p(i)-1 < p(i) < p(j)-1). But is p(i) guaranteed to be less than p(j)-1? Not necessarily. If p(i) > p(j)-1, then p(i) is not between p(i)-1 and p(j)-1. But they assume p(i)-1 < p(j)-1, then p(i) <= p(i)-1+1 = p(i). Actually p(i) = (p(i)-1)+1. So p(i) is exactly one column to the right of p(i)-1. So if p(i)-1 < p(j)-1, then p(i) <= p(j)-1? Not necessarily; p(i) could be larger than p(j)-1. For example, p(i)-1 = 5, p(i)=6, p(j)-1 = 7 (so p(j) = 8). Then p(i) = 6 is less than p(j)-1 = 7, so p(i) is between. But if p(i)-1 = 5, p(i)=6, p(j)-1 = 5 (impossible because p(i)-1 < p(j)-1). So p(i)-1 < p(j)-1 ensures p(i) <= p(j)-1? Actually p(i) = p(i)-1 + 1. Since p(i)-1 < p(j)-1, we have p(i) <= p(j)-1? Let's test: p(i)-1 = 5, p(j)-1 = 6 => p(i) = 6, p(j)-1 =6 => p(i)