                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Model the problem:** The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: one uncovered cell in each row and each column.  \n2. **Lower bound:**  \n   - In each row the covered cells form at most two contiguous segments (left of the hole and right of the hole).  \n   - The rightmost column of the left segment in row $i$ is $c_i-1$, where $c_i$ is the column of the hole in that row. Since the $c_i$ are all distinct, these rightmost columns are distinct, forcing at least one tile for each left segment.  \n   - Exactly one row can have $c_i=1$, so there are at least $2025-1$ left‑segment tiles.  \n   - An analogous argument for the right segments (using the leftmost column $c_i+1$) yields at least $2025-1$ right‑segment tiles.  \n   - No tile can cover a left segment of one row and a right segment of another row because any such rectangle would contain a hole. Hence at least $(2025-1)+(2025-1)=2\\cdot2025-2$ tiles are necessary.  \n3. **Upper bound (construction):**  \n   - Choose the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. the hole in row $i$ is at column $i$ for $i=1,\\dots,2025$.  \n   - For each row $i>1$ place a $1\\times(i-1)$ tile covering the cells $(i,1),(i,2),\\dots,(i,i-1)$.  \n   - For each row $i<2025$ place a $1\\times(2025-i)$ tile covering the cells $(i,i+1),\\dots,(i,2025)$.  \n   - These tiles are disjoint, cover all cells except the diagonal holes, and use exactly $(2025-1)+(2025-1)=2\\cdot2025-2$ tiles.  \n4. **Conclusion:** The lower and upper bounds coincide, so the minimal number of tiles is $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$.  \nDenote the rows and columns of the $n\\times n$ board by $\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}$.  \nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation $\\pi$ of $\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ such that the uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $\\pi(i)$.\n\nFor a fixed permutation $\\pi$ we must tile all remaining cells (the *covered* cells) with axis‑parallel rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines, no two rectangles overlapping.  \nA rectangle is determined by a pair of intervals\n\\[\nR=[r_1,r_2]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nC=[c_1,c_2]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nand consists of all cells $(i,j)$ with $i\\in R$ and $j\\in C$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nIn row $i$ the uncovered cell is at column $\\pi(i)$.  \nHence the covered cells in that row are the two (possibly empty) intervals\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j):\\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus each row contributes at most one *left* interval $L_i$ and at most one *right* interval $R_i$.\nIf $\\pi(i)=1$ then $L_i$ is empty; if $\\pi(i)=n$ then $R_i$ is empty.\nBecause $\\pi$ is a permutation, exactly one row has $\\pi(i)=1$ and exactly one row has $\\pi(i)=n$.\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{i:L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\}=n-1,\\qquad\n\\#\\{i:R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\}=n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsider a left interval $L_i$ with $\\pi(i)>1$.  \nIts rightmost column is $c_i:=\\pi(i)-1$.  \nSince the numbers $\\pi(1),\\dots ,\\pi(n)$ are all distinct, the values $c_i$ for the rows with $L_i\\neq\\varnothing$ are also all distinct.\nA rectangle covering any cell of $L_i$ cannot extend beyond column $c_i$, otherwise it would contain the hole $(i,\\pi(i))$.\nHence the rectangle that contains the cell $(i,c_i)$ must have its **right side** exactly at column $c_i$.\nBecause a rectangle has a unique right side, distinct left intervals require distinct rectangles.\nThus at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed to cover all non‑empty left intervals.\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the right intervals $R_i$ shows that at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed to cover all non‑empty right intervals:\nthe leftmost column of $R_i$ is $\\pi(i)+1$, these numbers are distinct, and a rectangle covering the cell $(i,\\pi(i)+1)$ must have its **left side** at that column.\n\nFinally, a single rectangle cannot contain a left interval of one row and a right interval of another row: any such rectangle would have to cross the hole of at least one of the two rows, which is forbidden.  \nHence the rectangles covering left intervals are disjoint from those covering right intervals, and the total number $T$ of rectangles satisfies\n\n\\[\nT\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound (construction)\n\nChoose the permutation $\\pi(i)=i$ (the identity).  \nThus the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells $(i,i)$, $i=1,\\dots ,n$.\n\nFor each row $i>1$ place a horizontal $1\\times(i-1)$ tile covering the cells\n\\[\n(i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1),\n\\]\ni.e. the whole left interval $L_i$.\nFor each row $i<n$ place a horizontal $1\\times(n-i)$ tile covering the cells\n\\[\n(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n),\n\\]\ni.e. the whole right interval $R_i$.\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows, and together they cover every cell of the board except the diagonal holes.  \nThe number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left intervals}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right intervals}}=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nEquation (2) gives a universal lower bound $2n-2$ for any admissible placement of uncovered squares, while equation (3) exhibits a placement attaining this bound.  \nTherefore $2n-2$ is the minimal possible number of tiles.\n\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Answer\n\nThe minimum number of rec