            "content": "final answer.**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Observation – permutation of uncovered squares:**  \n   The condition “exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column’’ forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix; i.e. there is a bijection \\(f:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the only uncovered squares are \\((i,f(i))\\).\n\n2. **Geometric reformulation:**  \n   The covered squares are the complement of this permutation matrix.  \n   Any axis‑parallel rectangle that avoids the uncovered squares must lie **entirely** either strictly above the diagonal (\\(i<j\\)) or strictly below it (\\(i>j\\)).  \n   Hence the problem splits into two independent subproblems: partition the strictly upper‑triangular part and the strictly lower‑triangular part of an \\(n\\times n\\) board into disjoint rectangles.\n\n3. **Upper‑triangular lower bound (Lemma 1):**  \n   Consider the \\(n-1\\) cells just above the diagonal: \\((i,i+1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   **Lemma 1** – *A rectangle contained in the upper‑triangular region can contain at most one of these cells.*  \n   Proof: If a rectangle contained \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), its row interval would have maximum index \\(\\ge j\\) while its column interval would have minimum index \\(\\le i+1\\); the required inequality “max row < min column’’ would be violated. ∎  \n   Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the upper‑triangular part.\n\n4. **Lower‑triangular lower bound (Lemma 2):**  \n   By symmetry, the \\(n-1\\) cells just below the diagonal \\((i,i-1)\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) each require a distinct rectangle. Hence at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the lower‑triangular part.\n\n5. **Combined lower bound:**  \n   Any tiling satisfying the conditions must use at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound (Upper bound):**  \n   Choose the identity permutation, i.e. the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a tile covering the cells \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\). This is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; the \\(n-1\\) tiles together cover the entire upper‑triangular region.  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place a tile covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\). This is a \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) rectangle; the \\(n-1\\) tiles together cover the lower‑triangular region.  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, use exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and leave precisely one uncovered square in each row and column.\n\n7. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow \\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by the tiles, and the tiles are axis‑parallel rectangles whose interiors contain no element of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of admissible rectangles  \n\nA rectangle is determined by a consecutive set of rows \\([a,b]\\) and a consecutive set of columns \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\)).  \nIf the rectangle contains a point \\((i,f(i))\\) of \\(U\\), then