1}^{t}1 = t.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).*  Permuting rows and columns does not change rank.  Multiplying \\(M\\) on the left by the permutation matrix \\(P_\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}\\) yields\n   \\[\n   P_\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}M = P_\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}J - I = J - I,\n   \\]\n   because \\(P_\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}J=J\\).  Hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)\\).\n\n   To find \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)\\), solve \\((J-I)x=0\\):\n   \\[\n   (J-I)x = (\\mathbf 1^{\\mathsf T}x)\\,\\mathbf 1 - x = 0\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   x = (\\mathbf 1^{\\mathsf T}x)\\,\\mathbf 1.\n   \\]\n   Thus every component of \\(x\\) equals the same scalar \\(c=\\mathbf 1^{\\mathsf T}x\\).  Substituting gives \\(c = n c\\), i.e. \\((n-1)c=0\\).  Since \\(n>1\\), we obtain \\(c=0\\) and consequently \\(x=0\\).  Therefore \\(J-I\\) is invertible, so\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\n   \\]\n\n   *Conclusion of the lower bound.*  From (1) we have \\(t\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\).  Thus any admissible placement uses at least \\(n=2025\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Matching bounds.**  The construction uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles, and the rank argument shows that fewer than \\(n\\) tiles are impossible.  Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a function\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is bijective; hence it is a permutation.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nthe positions of the \\(1\\)\u2019s of the